{"reference":"Romans 10","book":"Romans","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Scripture and the Near Word","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"In verses 5–17, Paul adapts Deuteronomy’s language about ascent, descent, and the word being near, connecting it with the Messiah, confession, trust, proclamation, and the one Lord shared by Jew and Greek. This is creative Jewish scriptural reasoning, not a simple contrast between an impossible Torah and an accessible gospel.","verse_refs":["Romans 10:5-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_010_262d6bf4a4af833a3c23ade7","reader_path_romans_010_de6237e3c06913db2c4b3d10"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Romans 10:13 uses Joel’s promise that everyone who calls on the Lord’s name will be saved. Paul places that wording within his argument about Israel and the confession of Jesus as Lord; it should therefore be read as part of that larger argument rather than detached from it.","verse_refs":["Romans 10:13","Romans 10:5-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_010_5ddef1603078de5864a88e96"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The beautiful feet of good-news messengers in verse 15 most directly echoes Isaiah 52:7. Nahum 1:15 offers a related image of a messenger bringing good news, but it is a parallel rather than Paul’s direct source.","verse_refs":["Romans 10:15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_010_5a0da4f0a6ea0e8f3b98b066"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Romans 10 in Its Larger Argument","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Romans 10 belongs to Romans 9–11, an integral part of the letter’s gospel argument. Across these chapters, Israel’s privileges, remnant, future hope, beloved status, and irrevocable gifts are repeatedly affirmed. Paul’s grief and prayer for Israel at the opening of this chapter should be heard within that continuing concern.","verse_refs":["Romans 11:1-36","Romans 12:1-21","Romans 13:1-14","Romans 14:1-23","Romans 15:1-33","Romans 16:1-27","Romans 1:1-32","Romans 2:1-29","Romans 3:1-31","Romans 4:1-25","Romans 5:1-21","Romans 5:17-21","Romans 6:1-23","Romans 7:1-25","Romans 8:1-39","Romans 9:1-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_010_2f3b378e374df7b9e5091e7a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The surrounding discussion uses related but distinct categories—Israel, Jew, gentile, circumcision, uncircumcision, Abrahamic children, remnant, branches, and the Messiah assembly. Keeping those distinctions in view prevents gentile inclusion from becoming identity erasure, replacement ownership, or collective Jewish blame. It also cautions against automatically reading “Christ is the end of the law” in Romans 10:4 as Torah abolition or as a claim that Judaism persists only through stubborn legalism.","verse_refs":["Romans 11:1-32","Romans 9:1-33","Romans 9:30-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_010_f76f22048e918526152c3fab"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why This Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Paul’s use of Joel matters because he applies a promise about calling on the Lord’s name to confession of Jesus as Lord while his argument remains centrally concerned with Israel. That setting guards against treating the quotation as though it erased Israel or its continuing Jewish interpretation.","verse_refs":["Romans 10:13","Romans 10:5-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_010_3b9943d2cd6f41acda9b3caf"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}